resource_tool_mirrors — umbrella (DECIDED 2026-07-15, planned;
AMENDED 2026-07-30 — the mirror set is an instance of an INVARIANT)
==============================================================

status: planned (2026-07-15)
decided: 2026-07-15
wave: TBD
amended: 2026-07-30 — the rule is "every declared resource is reachable as
a tool"; the six-tool list below is that rule evaluated against the
resources that existed on 2026-07-15. Mechanism for the open half
PENDING (Q4).
spec: "resource-read tool mirrors" under "mcp resources", including
its "every declared resource is reachable as a tool" subsection.
per-tool plans: plans/read_theory, plans/read_commands,
plans/read_diagnostics, plans/list_entities, plans/read_session,
plans/read_named.

why (the two operating assumptions)
-----------------------------------

- PRIMARY (human-in-the-loop): the main usage, ~1-year horizon, is a
  human directing the model through claude code; the user @-mentions
  resources when needed. autonomous resource-reading is NOT required
  for this mode.
- SECONDARY — RESOLVED POSITIVE (2026-07-15, tested against claude
  code; this plan carried the stale "UNVERIFIED" wording until
  2026-07-30): claude code DOES let the model enumerate and read
  resources autonomously, mid-turn, with no user @-mention —
  ListMcpResourcesTool + two ReadMcpResourceTool reads
  (isabelle://session, isabelle://named/greeting) all returned
  content. this RELIEVES but does not eliminate the case for tool
  twins: they are still required for clients that surface resources to
  the HUMAN only, and for a mode that hides the resource surface and
  wants the reads as tools the model is nudged to call. see Q1
  (closed).

the invariant (added 2026-07-30 — read this before the list)
------------------------------------------------------------

every resource the server DECLARES must also be readable through a
tool: same content, same handler. "declares" includes what USERS
declare, which is what changed — parameterised resources (spec,
2026-07-28) made mcp_resource a real extension point with typed params
and a FREE uri namespace, so the readable-resource set is now an
open-ended REGISTRY and a literal six-name list cannot track it.

two breakages this plan must fix, not merely note:

- read_named {name} is strictly LESS capable than its resource. no
  argument slot in the schema, so a resource declared with a params
  clause (goal_hints{?depth}, readable at
  isabelle://named/goal_hints?depth=5) is tool-readable only at its
  defaults. see plans/read_named, which is BLOCKED on Q4.
- a resource in the free namespace (outside theory/ repl/ session
  named/) has no twin at all and nothing notices.

no-drift rule, both directions: a twin must never GROW behavior its
resource lacks (the original rule) and must never LACK behavior its
resource HAS (added 2026-07-30; the direction that actually broke).

terminology: TOOL TWIN for the resource→tool relation. a builtin's ML
MIRROR ROW (activation registry shadowing a scala row) is a different
relation; the six below keep "mirror" in their names since renaming
landed plans is churn.

the shipped set (the invariant evaluated on 2026-07-15)
------------------------------------------------------

  read_theory      {name, lines?}            = isabelle://theory/{name}
  read_commands    {theory, start?, count?}  = .../commands
  read_diagnostics {theory}                  = .../diagnostics
  list_entities    {theory, kind?, prefix?}  = .../entities
  read_session     {}                        = isabelle://session
  read_named       {name}                    = isabelle://named/{name}

these six stay HAND-WRITTEN whatever Q4 decides: five of them resolve
tiers through Sessions.load_structure / deps / Store, which has no ML
equivalent, so they cannot be generated from an ML registry.

already covered, NO twin added: isabelle://repl/{id} and .../text are
already served by the repl_show / repl_text builtins (same Ir.show /
Ir.text backing).

shared design
-------------

1. one function, two surfaces. factor each uri-read handler so the tool
   and the resource call the SAME code. a mirror must never grow
   behavior its resource lacks — that is how the surfaces drift. this
   is the load-bearing invariant of the whole feature; every per-tool
   plan restates it and every per-tool test pins parity.
2. no new backend: theory-name normalization (image_theory), tier
   resolution, lazy/truncation/slicing all reused as-is.
3. activation (tools half): the mirrors are builtin tools, so they get
   mirror rows in the ML registry (the "builtin tools in the activation
   layer" drift gate REQUIRES them) and obey [[mcp_tools add/del]] /
   bundles. ship REGISTERED-BUT-INACTIVE so human-in-the-loop
   tools/list stays uncluttered. one bundle activates the set:

     bundle autonomous_proving = [[mcp_tools add: read_theory
       read_commands read_diagnostics list_entities read_session
       read_named]]

   the theory carrying this bundle is designated as the agent context
   (tool_scope) for an autonomous-proving run.
4. resource suppression (resources half — MECHANISM OPEN): no existing
   mechanism hides templates (resource scope is scope_add over theory
   names; templates are not per-name entities). likely shape: a
   disposition on the designated agent-context theory that makes
   resources/list + resources/templates/list return empty. SETTLE HERE
   before implementing — this is the one genuinely new mechanism.

5. twins for USER-DECLARED resources (added 2026-07-30, MECHANISM
   OPEN — Q4). the six above cover the server-shipped resources; the
   ML resource registry is open-ended and needs its own answer. do NOT
   implement one of these before Q4 is settled:

   (a) read_named grows {name, args} with args an untyped blob. one
       tool, no registry walk. AGAINST (strongest single argument in
       the set): an untyped args blob is precisely what
       plans/param_schema_v2 exists to abolish, and resources reuse the
       tool param machinery so their arguments ARE typed. also leaves
       the free namespace uncovered.
   (b) a generic read_resource {uri} routing through
       mcp_resource_read. covers query params in one row; same shape as
       claude code's ReadMcpResourceTool. AGAINST: uri construction
       moves to the model, no per-resource schema, and coverage is
       bounded by the spec's ROUTING GAP — the free namespace returns
       "Unknown MCP resource" exactly as resources/read does.
   (c) GENERATED twins, projecting each registry entry into a tool row
       (resource params ARE tool params). FOR: ML resources are
       addressed by (name, args), never by template matching, so a
       generated twin dispatches straight to MCP.read_resource and
       reads the free namespace resources/read cannot route — it closes
       the routing gap from the tool side without giving ML a template
       matcher, and needs no enumerator (a twin advertises a schema,
       not instances). AGAINST: generated names land in the TOOL
       namespace, so the free resource namespace can collide with a
       builtin name (a user resource called load_theory) and a
       collision rule is needed; covers the ML half only.

   fourth discriminator, ACTIVATION — it cuts against (c) and item 3's
   bundle is where it bites: `bundle autonomous_proving = [[mcp_tools
   add: read_theory ... read_named]]` is a literal six-name list, the
   very thing the invariant says cannot track a growing registry. under
   (b) the bundle just gains read_resource. under (c) the bundle cannot
   NAME a twin that does not exist until a user declares its resource,
   while item 3 ships twins registered-but-INACTIVE — so a generated
   twin arrives inactive with nothing able to activate it. fixing that
   means either set-valued activation ("all resource twins", a kind
   [[mcp_tools add: ...]] lacks today) or generated twins active by
   default, which reopens the tools/list clutter inactive-by-default
   exists to prevent. settle it WITH Q4, not after.

   whichever wins, the twin must reuse MCP_Resource's validate /
   check_value — no second copy of the param machinery, same rule
   param_schema_v2 sets.

open questions
--------------

- Q1: CLOSED 2026-07-15 — claude code DOES read resources
  autonomously (see the SECONDARY assumption above). the mirror
  decision stands regardless; the resolution relieves urgency, it does
  not remove the requirement.
- Q2: resource-suppression mechanism (item 4).
- Q3: wave slotting in the spec implementation order (left open per the
  user's "we now need" without a when).
- Q4 (added 2026-07-30, BLOCKS plans/read_named and any twin for
  user-declared resources): which mechanism from item 5 — (a) args
  passthrough, (b) generic read_resource, (c) generated twins? depends
  on the param-schema work landing (all three want ptyp/validate) and,
  for (c), on a tool-namespace collision rule. the six hand-written
  twins are NOT blocked on this.

tests (shape; per-tool plans carry the specifics)
-------------------------------------------------

- [scala unit] each twin: row + dispatch + PARITY with its resource
  read over Fake_Backend.
- [e2e] read by tool and by resource, assert byte-identical payloads.
- [drift gate] each new builtin has its ML mirror row (existing
  builtin_activation gate covers this once the tools land).
- [drift gate, the invariant's own — added 2026-07-30] declared-
  resource set ≡ resource-read-tool set, BOTH directions: a resource
  with no twin fails, a twin whose resource is gone fails. it must read
  the two registries, NOT a literal name list — a literal list is the
  failure mode the spec already indicts for
  MCP_Server.resource_templates ("advertised before their backing
  exists, and with NO drift gate"). shape: the builtin drift gate over
  the live bridge. cannot be written until Q4 fixes what the twin set
  is; until then this is an obligation, not a test.
- [parity, params direction — added 2026-07-30] for a parameterised
  resource, a twin call passing a non-default argument must return the
  SAME payload as the equivalent uri read (goal_hints depth=5 vs
  isabelle://named/goal_hints?depth=5). this is the case that pins the
  no-LACK direction of the drift rule.
